---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { links, url } from '../lib/links';

const questions = [
  { area: 'Sales', items: ['Which customers ordered less this quarter than last?', 'What is still open on our ten largest orders, and when is it due?'] },
  { area: 'Purchasing', items: ['Which purchase orders are late, and from which suppliers?', 'Which items have we bought from more than one supplier this year, and at what prices?'] },
  { area: 'Service', items: ['Which service orders have been open longest, and for which customers?', 'Which items come back for repair most often?'] },
  { area: 'Manufacturing', items: ['Which work orders are behind schedule this week?', 'Which components are short for the orders due next month?'] },
  { area: 'Finance', items: ['Which customers have invoices more than 60 days overdue?', 'How do this month’s sales compare with the same month last year, by product group?'] },
];

const summary = `mcp-server-db2i is an open-source MCP server (MIT) that lets AI assistants such as Claude and Cursor read Db2 for i.
- Read-only: a read-only connection, a SQL validator and a query timeout that cancels on the IBM i.
- People sign in with their own IBM i profile (OAuth), so object authority and exit programs apply as usual.
- A library allowlist, column masking and an audit log of every tool call.
- Connects with the IBM i Access ODBC driver, JT400, or Mapepire over SSH. Nothing is installed on the IBM i.
Quickstart: ${links.quickstart}
Security: ${links.security}`;
---
<Base
  title="For business teams"
  description="Ask new questions of the ERP data on your IBM i in plain language, alongside the screens, reports, Excel workbooks and BI tools you already use."
  starterQuestions={[
    'What does my IBM i team need to set up?',
    'How is our data kept read-only and private?',
    'Can people sign in with their own IBM i user?',
  ]}
>

  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="label">For business teams</p>
      <h1>For the questions that don't have a report yet.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        If your ERP runs on IBM i, years of orders, customers, items and invoices are already there.
        Your team can ask about them in plain language, in an AI assistant such as Claude, and get the answer
        from live data. It works alongside the reports and tools you use today.
      </p>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href={links.docs} data-ask-docs="business-hero">Ask the docs</a>
        <a class="link-rule" href="#send-to-it">Send this to your IBM i team</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <p class="label">What you have</p>
        <div>
          <h2>It works with what you already have.</h2>
          <p class="lead">Most companies with an ERP on IBM i get their answers in one of three ways. Each does its job well, and each was built with care by people who know the business.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cols3">
        <div><p class="label">01</p><h3>ERP screens and reports</h3><p class="muted">The system your team works in every day, with the reports that were built for it.</p></div>
        <div><p class="label">02</p><h3>Excel over ODBC</h3><p class="muted">Workbooks that pull straight from the ERP, shaped by someone who knows exactly which fields matter.</p></div>
        <div><p class="label">03</p><h3>A warehouse or BI tool</h3><p class="muted">Dashboards and reports on data that has been modeled for the questions the business asks most.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="bridge">
        Then there is the question that comes up on a Tuesday afternoon and doesn't have a report yet.
        That is where this fits in. It doesn't replace any of the above. It gives you a way to ask the one-off question
        straight away, against live data, and it reuses what your existing reports and queries already know.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <p class="label">Examples</p>
        <div>
          <h2>The kind of questions people ask.</h2>
          <p class="lead">Your team types a question. The assistant finds the right tables, writes a read-only query, and answers with the numbers.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="groups">
        {questions.map((q) => (
          <div class="group">
            <p class="label">{q.area}</p>
            <ul class="rows">{q.items.map((item) => <li>“{item}”</li>)}</ul>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container two">
      <div>
        <p class="label">Definitions</p>
        <h2 class="h2s">Your definitions, not the model's guesses.</h2>
        <p>
          Every company has its own meaning of “open order”, “active customer” or “on time”. Your IBM i team can write
          those definitions down once, in a plain text file: what a status code means, how two tables join, which query
          answers “late purchase orders”.
        </p>
        <p>
          The assistant then uses your definitions instead of guessing. The logic that already lives in your reports and
          Excel queries is a good place to start.
        </p>
        <a class="link-rule" href={links.customTools}>How business definitions work <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="label">Safeguards</p>
        <h2 class="h2s">Why your IBM i team can say yes.</h2>
        <ul class="rows ticks">
          <li><strong>Read-only at three levels.</strong> A read-only connection, a check that only allows queries, and a time limit that stops long queries on the IBM i.</li>
          <li><strong>Everyone signs in as themselves.</strong> People use their own IBM i user, so they see exactly what they can see today.</li>
          <li><strong>You choose what is visible.</strong> Only the libraries you allow, with sensitive columns hidden or masked.</li>
          <li><strong>Everything is logged.</strong> Every request is written to an audit log.</li>
          <li><strong>The data stays on your IBM i.</strong> Nothing is copied into a new database.</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="link-rule" href={links.security}>Security details <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <p class="label">Good to know</p>
        <div><h2>Before you start.</h2></div>
      </div>
      <div class="cols3">
        <div><p class="label">Setup</p><h3>Someone sets it up once</h3><p class="muted">Your IBM i team installs and configures the server. After that, people only add a link in their assistant and sign in.</p></div>
        <div><p class="label">Scope</p><h3>It is not your financial reporting</h3><p class="muted">Official figures still come from the reports your finance team relies on. This is for exploring and for the questions in between.</p></div>
        <div><p class="label">Quality</p><h3>Better notes, better answers</h3><p class="muted">The assistant is only as good as what it knows about your tables. A few definitions make a big difference.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight" id="send-to-it">
    <div class="container two next">
      <div>
        <p class="label">Next step</p>
        <h2 class="h2s">Share it with your IBM i team.</h2>
        <p>
          Ask the documentation assistant anything about setup, security or what it can do. Or send the summary on the
          right to your IBM i team. It is free and open source, with nothing to buy.
        </p>
        <div class="btn-row">
          <a class="btn btn--primary" href={links.docs} data-ask-docs="business-next">Ask the docs</a>
          <a class="link-rule" href={url('/blog')}>Read the blog</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="panel share">
        <div class="share-head">
          <span class="label">For your IBM i team</span>
          <button type="button" class="copy" data-copy-summary>Copy</button>
        </div>
        <pre id="summary">{summary}</pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<script>
  const button = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-copy-summary]');
  button?.addEventListener('click', async () => {
    const text = document.getElementById('summary')?.textContent ?? '';
    try {
      await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);
      button.textContent = 'Copied';
    } catch {
      button.textContent = 'Select and copy';
    }
    setTimeout(() => (button.textContent = 'Copy'), 1600);
  });
</script>

<style>
  .hero { padding-block: 112px 72px; }
  .hero h1 { margin: 18px 0 28px; max-width: 16ch; }
  .hero .lead { margin-bottom: 36px; }
  .cols3 { display: grid; border-top: 1px solid var(--line-strong); }
  @media (min-width: 900px) { .cols3 { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); } }
  .cols3 > div { display: grid; gap: 10px; align-content: start; padding: 22px 0 28px; }
  .cols3 > div + div { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .cols3 > div { padding-right: 28px; }
    .cols3 > div + div { border-top: 0; border-left: 1px solid var(--line); padding-left: 28px; }
  }
  .cols3 h3 { margin: 0; font-size: 1.4rem; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
  .cols3 p.muted { margin: 0; }
  .bridge { max-width: 52ch; margin-top: var(--space-7); font-size: 1.3rem; line-height: 1.45; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
  .groups { border-top: 1px solid var(--line-strong); }
  .group { display: grid; gap: 8px 64px; padding-block: 20px 24px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  @media (min-width: 1000px) { .group { grid-template-columns: 200px minmax(0, 1fr); } }
  .group .rows li { padding: 8px 0; font-size: 1.1rem; }
  .group .rows li:first-child { padding-top: 0; }
  .two { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--space-7); border-top: 1px solid var(--line-strong); padding-top: var(--space-6); }
  @media (min-width: 900px) { .two { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 64px; } }
  .two > div > .label { margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .h2s { font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3.4vw, 2.6rem); max-width: 18ch; margin-bottom: var(--space-5); }
  .two p { max-width: 58ch; }
  .ticks { margin: 0 0 var(--space-5); }
  .ticks li { padding: 12px 0 12px 22px; position: relative; }
  .ticks li::before { content: ''; position: absolute; left: 0; top: 20px; width: 7px; height: 7px; background: var(--accent); }
  .share { align-self: start; }
  .share-head { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .copy { font: 500 11px/1 var(--font-mono); letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; background: var(--fg); color: var(--bg); border: 0; border-radius: var(--radius-btn); padding: 8px 12px; cursor: pointer; }
  .copy:hover { background: var(--accent); color: var(--on-accent); }
  .share pre { margin: 0; padding: 0; border: 0; background: none; white-space: pre-wrap; overflow-wrap: anywhere; font-size: 0.8rem; }
</style>
